[[:db/retract 17592186125085 :question/answer-fields 17592186123110] {:db/id 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-403fa166-7921-5239-b399-2bac8767c7a0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124381 :question/problem "Find a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-4,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n![](images/e3/e38c742a22bea651a3c410744f798297306661b6eee2929d7c5efff3bdb78844.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathcal{B}= \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PDP^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order: \n\n![](images/eb/ebedba78b53f92ed5e4d69322a5e682675bdfc13b2687575fdf94f9c78a1cbe5.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/16/16933b8fd024b62ad8fafa36b382aefce74383a9bf47b952bf7e0e12fe26cae6.svg) Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124339 :question/problem "Find $A^{100},$ where \n![](images/81/81d78f5e129614d85d3719539ab9afc1a04307afb3b03eb9d7a55829ee6354c9.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First,  notice that\n\n![](images/43/43438bad181467e28a2e0758f7b7aa070fd5f5de37e1a6e19d4cabb591dc4f7d.svg)\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/93/93e5e9f9947fb4299da165192f103d7be7494ecc752edacf1a4931d4766337ef.svg)\n\nSince $A=PDP^{-1},$ we can compute $A^k$ by using the formula \n\n$$\nA^{k} = PD^{k}P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{100} & =P{D}^{100}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{100}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{100} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {0}^{100}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-44ed4b4e-a305-51c9-9d7d-957ba67e3cad"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124340 :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $E,$ given that \n![](images/06/063c1f52097f096bc39a2f8ba29f251c655c926a542828dfba50bcd3bb29dcea.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/84/84fd9d7243958aa329a23c3c9f19e7c35b8969699511b72bdfc4546b4577fc4a.svg)\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=6$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n![](images/fd/fd405e80c68f63df281c9f3e2fe4dd8ab25e32a0214619a7e5220c7e01944ab8.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354491 :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -15 \\\\ 12 & -30\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-403fa166-7921-5239-b399-2bac8767c7a0" :question/id #uuid "403fa166-7921-5239-b399-2bac8767c7a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-86678" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $S$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $S,$ then $S$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $S=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $S,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nS={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}40 & -2 \\\\ 35 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & -56 \\\\ 49 & -51\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6a4b5644-3578-5ae2-81ef-1e895ed96291"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6a4b5644-3578-5ae2-81ef-1e895ed96291" :answer-field/id #uuid "6a4b5644-3578-5ae2-81ef-1e895ed96291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-63ef4572-30b7-56ef-8f5a-ac03a89540d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5e3acd5e-d728-5511-ba7b-b5202ac2ddfb" "answer-271153a7-e008-5233-9e22-3f12deb489c2" "answer-63ef4572-30b7-56ef-8f5a-ac03a89540d1" "answer-7154f91d-3460-5bf3-bea9-dc1e61a7a77f" "answer-57dd64ab-9583-5552-b2ec-3be32f9e1309"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e3acd5e-d728-5511-ba7b-b5202ac2ddfb" :answer/id #uuid "5e3acd5e-d728-5511-ba7b-b5202ac2ddfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ 9 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-271153a7-e008-5233-9e22-3f12deb489c2" :answer/id #uuid "271153a7-e008-5233-9e22-3f12deb489c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63ef4572-30b7-56ef-8f5a-ac03a89540d1" :answer/id #uuid "63ef4572-30b7-56ef-8f5a-ac03a89540d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}54 & -56 \\\\ 49 & -51\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7154f91d-3460-5bf3-bea9-dc1e61a7a77f" :answer/id #uuid "7154f91d-3460-5bf3-bea9-dc1e61a7a77f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}54 & -56 \\\\ 51 & -49\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57dd64ab-9583-5552-b2ec-3be32f9e1309" :answer/id #uuid "57dd64ab-9583-5552-b2ec-3be32f9e1309" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}56 & -54 \\\\ 49 & -51\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-44ed4b4e-a305-51c9-9d7d-957ba67e3cad" :question/id #uuid "44ed4b4e-a305-51c9-9d7d-957ba67e3cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-37605" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ef6614e3-dc36-51f8-9c16-862fd38745d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ef6614e3-dc36-51f8-9c16-862fd38745d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ef6614e3-dc36-51f8-9c16-862fd38745d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f3482122-1b23-51b6-bab9-0274990d6acf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f3482122-1b23-51b6-bab9-0274990d6acf" "answer-b20dbe0a-b3d3-556e-baa9-349554260ed9" "answer-26a52f5c-fb5a-567f-a05c-b4a3377da12a" "answer-65b8a476-fed9-5e7d-acb6-3aec390dd318" "answer-349ea5d5-1623-5b94-b22b-a5a6bd4fb201"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3482122-1b23-51b6-bab9-0274990d6acf" :answer/id #uuid "f3482122-1b23-51b6-bab9-0274990d6acf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b20dbe0a-b3d3-556e-baa9-349554260ed9" :answer/id #uuid "b20dbe0a-b3d3-556e-baa9-349554260ed9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26a52f5c-fb5a-567f-a05c-b4a3377da12a" :answer/id #uuid "26a52f5c-fb5a-567f-a05c-b4a3377da12a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65b8a476-fed9-5e7d-acb6-3aec390dd318" :answer/id #uuid "65b8a476-fed9-5e7d-acb6-3aec390dd318" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-349ea5d5-1623-5b94-b22b-a5a6bd4fb201" :answer/id #uuid "349ea5d5-1623-5b94-b22b-a5a6bd4fb201" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125791 :tutorial/content "Remember that the diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ and corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ is\n![](images/cd/cde63e04857414720bed0cb247e1e0c4bae76b69823f4aeb494cda13c139ac13.svg)\n\nThis idea also works in reverse. Given a diagonalization $A$ in the form \n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe can immediately deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are the diagonal entries of $D,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of $P.$ This is because the diagonalization of a matrix is (almost) unique.\n\n**Note:** The diagonalization is \"almost\" unique because the order of the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs may vary, and the eigenvectors may be replaced with scalar multiples of themselves. However, none of these variations change the actual outcome regarding the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs that we deduce." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125790 :tutorial/content "One useful property of diagonalization is that it allows us to easily determine the power of a matrix.\n\nAs an example, we consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix ![](images/53/53b7a9efd9f2ae1ef4adc7cee5f86f784722777b8c29f268dbd0e822ff1354f9.svg) with the diagonalization\n![](images/e3/e3111a91b80d238cf5255eb9a9884dcd3f80e914039c5e789e32c07b55cf5b06.svg)\n\nWe will use the diagonalization of $A$ to compute $A^{55}.$\n\nLet's start by computing $A^2.$ Using the properties of matrix multiplication, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =PD{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =PD\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P(DD){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $A^3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot A \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P({D}^{2}D){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{3}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix $P$ and its inverse $P^{-1}$ will always cancel each other out in the middle of the multiplication! So by induction, we have that\n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}55}=P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}55} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we only need to find $D^{\\color{blue}55}.$\n\nSince $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {D}^{3} & ={D}^{2}D=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing this pattern, we get\n\n![](images/05/05e10fdc6944077a48b0214a41fae8d418a1b66c8d87a47d652158c84285c246.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{55} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{55}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given that $A = PDP^{-1},$ for any non-negative integer ${\\color{blue}k},$ we obtain \n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}k} = P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}k} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124754 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
